 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Easy Aerial, a New York-based developer of autonomous tethered drone systems for aerial surveillance, raised $20 million in Series B funding from Insight Partners and Entrée Capital .

 Why it's the BFD: The startup was at the center of controversy earlier this year, when it was booted from the Brooklyn Navy Yard.

 Easy Aerial said the eviction was prompted by protests over its work with the Israeli Defense Forces, with its CEO claiming that Mayor Zohran Mamdani was behind the decision. Navy Yard officials claimed that Easy Aerial's lease wasn't renewed because of "repeated compliance" violations, including "flying drones in unauthorized areas." The company now appears to be based in a different Brooklyn commercial complex. The bottom line: "Two of the company's multi-rotor drones are on the Defense Department's Blue UAS Cleared List, which means they meet certain congressionally mandated requirements and are therefore eligible for purchase by DoD and federal agencies." — Cal Biesecker, Defense Daily 

 
 
